\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces  Proxy-fluid versus free-streaming dark radiation. In the effective linear fluid description, the PTUM proxy component has $w_\psi =1/3$, $c_{s,\psi }^2=1/3$, and $\sigma _\psi =0$, and therefore does not induce the neutrino-like CMB acoustic phase shift characteristic of free-streaming relativistic species. The key discriminator is whether an extra relativistic component produces a measurable acoustic phase shift, not only whether it contributes to the radiation density. }}{35}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ptum_proxy_fluid_vs_free_streaming}{{2}{35}{Proxy-fluid versus free-streaming dark radiation. In the effective linear fluid description, the PTUM proxy component has $w_\psi =1/3$, $c_{s,\psi }^2=1/3$, and $\sigma _\psi =0$, and therefore does not induce the neutrino-like CMB acoustic phase shift characteristic of free-streaming relativistic species. The key discriminator is whether an extra relativistic component produces a measurable acoustic phase shift, not only whether it contributes to the radiation density}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces PTUM predictions in the \(n_s\)--\(r\) plane using the exact-background calculation. Blue bands indicate the Planck-preferred region; the green shading marks the BICEP/Keck upper bound on \(r\). For \(\lambda \gtrsim 0.02\), PTUM lies in or very near the observationally allowed region.}}{36}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:nsr_exact}{{3}{36}{PTUM predictions in the \(n_s\)--\(r\) plane using the exact-background calculation. Blue bands indicate the Planck-preferred region; the green shading marks the BICEP/Keck upper bound on \(r\). For \(\lambda \gtrsim 0.02\), PTUM lies in or very near the observationally allowed region}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Comparison of exact-background and slow-roll primordial predictions across the full \(\lambda \) scan. The slow-roll approximation underestimates \(n_s\) by \(\sim 5\times 10^{-4}\) and overestimates \(r\) by \(\sim 1.4\%\), confirming its quantitative reliability for the PTUM creation sector.}}{37}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:exactvssr}{{4}{37}{Comparison of exact-background and slow-roll primordial predictions across the full \(\lambda \) scan. The slow-roll approximation underestimates \(n_s\) by \(\sim 5\times 10^{-4}\) and overestimates \(r\) by \(\sim 1.4\%\), confirming its quantitative reliability for the PTUM creation sector}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Characteristic PTUM creation scales inferred from the observed scalar amplitude. The PTUM creation event lies in a GUT-adjacent energy band, well below the Planck scale.}}{37}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:creation}{{5}{37}{Characteristic PTUM creation scales inferred from the observed scalar amplitude. The PTUM creation event lies in a GUT-adjacent energy band, well below the Planck scale}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Viable-region scan of the PTUM dark-energy sector. Left: best achievable \(\log _{10}(1+w_{\rm  DE})\) in the \((\kappa ,\lambda _{\rm  res})\) plane. Right: corresponding optimal field value \(\Phi _{\rm  res}\). The wide viable region shows that near-\(\Lambda \) behavior is not confined to a fine-tuned point. \emph  {(Analytic scan, author-reported; script pending release.)}}}{37}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:DEviable}{{6}{37}{Viable-region scan of the PTUM dark-energy sector. Left: best achievable \(\log _{10}(1+w_{\rm DE})\) in the \((\kappa ,\lambda _{\rm res})\) plane. Right: corresponding optimal field value \(\Phi _{\rm res}\). The wide viable region shows that near-\(\Lambda \) behavior is not confined to a fine-tuned point. \emph {(Analytic scan, author-reported; script pending release.)}}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The PTUM dark-sector evolution function \(\gamma (z)\) compared with the CLASS numerical solution. The analytic form~\eqref  {eq:gamma} is reported to match the Boltzmann output within machine precision across the full redshift range shown. \textbf  {Note:} This figure is author-reported as regenerated from the final CLASS/Cobaya chain release with the best-fit value \(c=0.3854\), \(\gamma (0)=0.722\) reported in the text; the chains are not yet public.}}{37}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gamma}{{7}{37}{The PTUM dark-sector evolution function \(\gamma (z)\) compared with the CLASS numerical solution. The analytic form~\eqref {eq:gamma} is reported to match the Boltzmann output within machine precision across the full redshift range shown. \textbf {Note:} This figure is author-reported as regenerated from the final CLASS/Cobaya chain release with the best-fit value \(c=0.3854\), \(\gamma (0)=0.722\) reported in the text; the chains are not yet public}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Raw CMB TT residuals (left) and matter-power residuals (right) for PTUM proxy-fluid dark radiation over \(\Delta N_{\rm  fluid}=0.01\)--\(0.20\). The response scales approximately linearly with \(\Delta N_{\rm  fluid}\). \emph  {(Author-reported figure; generated with the modified CLASS code, which is not yet public.)}}}{38}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:multiDN}{{8}{38}{Raw CMB TT residuals (left) and matter-power residuals (right) for PTUM proxy-fluid dark radiation over \(\Delta N_{\rm fluid}=0.01\)--\(0.20\). The response scales approximately linearly with \(\Delta N_{\rm fluid}\). \emph {(Author-reported figure; generated with the modified CLASS code, which is not yet public.)}}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Fractional residuals of the CMB lensing potential power spectrum \(C_L^{\phi \phi }\) for PTUM relative to \(\Lambda \)CDM. The suppression grows with \(\Delta N_{\rm  fluid}\) and reaches \(\sim 5\%\) at \(L\sim 1000\) for \(\Delta N=0.31\). \emph  {(Author-reported figure; modified CLASS code not yet public.)}}}{38}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lensing_pp}{{9}{38}{Fractional residuals of the CMB lensing potential power spectrum \(C_L^{\phi \phi }\) for PTUM relative to \(\Lambda \)CDM. The suppression grows with \(\Delta N_{\rm fluid}\) and reaches \(\sim 5\%\) at \(L\sim 1000\) for \(\Delta N=0.31\). \emph {(Author-reported figure; modified CLASS code not yet public.)}}{figure.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces  CMB acoustic phase-shift diagnostic. A free-streaming relativistic component generates anisotropic stress and produces a neutrino-like displacement of the acoustic peak structure relative to the baseline spectrum. In contrast, the PTUM proxy-fluid component has $\sigma _\psi =0$ within the effective linear fluid approximation and therefore does not induce the same phase shift. The curves shown here are schematic diagnostics rather than numerical CLASS outputs. }}{39}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ptum_cmb_phase_shift_diagnostic}{{10}{39}{CMB acoustic phase-shift diagnostic. A free-streaming relativistic component generates anisotropic stress and produces a neutrino-like displacement of the acoustic peak structure relative to the baseline spectrum. In contrast, the PTUM proxy-fluid component has $\sigma _\psi =0$ within the effective linear fluid approximation and therefore does not induce the same phase shift. The curves shown here are schematic diagnostics rather than numerical CLASS outputs}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Posterior distributions from the full PTUM likelihood analysis. The pfd amplitude peaks near zero and has a broad upper tail, consistent with an upper-limit result. \emph  {(Author-reported; chains not yet public.)}}}{39}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ptumtriangle}{{11}{39}{Posterior distributions from the full PTUM likelihood analysis. The pfd amplitude peaks near zero and has a broad upper tail, consistent with an upper-limit result. \emph {(Author-reported; chains not yet public.)}}{figure.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Left: binned maximum-posterior (MAP) envelope of \(\Delta N_{\rm  fluid}\) constructed from the PTUM chains; this is a descriptive diagnostic, not a frequentist profile likelihood (see text). Right: marginal posterior PDF with 68\% and 95\% credible regions shaded. \emph  {(Author-reported; chains not yet public.)}}}{39}{figure.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:profile_dN}{{12}{39}{Left: binned maximum-posterior (MAP) envelope of \(\Delta N_{\rm fluid}\) constructed from the PTUM chains; this is a descriptive diagnostic, not a frequentist profile likelihood (see text). Right: marginal posterior PDF with 68\% and 95\% credible regions shaded. \emph {(Author-reported; chains not yet public.)}}{figure.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Detection capability matrix for PTUM observables with current and future experiments. Top left: tensor ratio \(r\) vs \(\lambda \) with detector reach bands; top right: projected \(\Delta N_{\rm  fluid}\) upper-limit improvement; bottom left: fluid--free-streaming phase-shift detectability; bottom right: summary detection matrix. \emph  {(FENOMENOLOJİK forecast; Fisher code not public.)}}}{40}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:forecast}{{13}{40}{Detection capability matrix for PTUM observables with current and future experiments. Top left: tensor ratio \(r\) vs \(\lambda \) with detector reach bands; top right: projected \(\Delta N_{\rm fluid}\) upper-limit improvement; bottom left: fluid--free-streaming phase-shift detectability; bottom right: summary detection matrix. \emph {(FENOMENOLOJİK forecast; Fisher code not public.)}}{figure.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces BBN consistency check for PTUM. The shaded region shows the BBN-allowed range $\Delta N_{\rm  eff}<0.40$ from primordial helium and deuterium abundances~\cite  {Fields2020,Cyburt2016}. The vertical line marks the PTUM 95\% upper limit $\Delta N_{\rm  fluid}<0.31$, which lies well within the BBN-allowed region. At the maximum allowed pfd abundance, the predicted shift in $Y_p$ is approximately $1.1\sigma $.}}{40}{figure.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bbn}{{14}{40}{BBN consistency check for PTUM. The shaded region shows the BBN-allowed range $\Delta N_{\rm eff}<0.40$ from primordial helium and deuterium abundances~\cite {Fields2020,Cyburt2016}. The vertical line marks the PTUM 95\% upper limit $\Delta N_{\rm fluid}<0.31$, which lies well within the BBN-allowed region. At the maximum allowed pfd abundance, the predicted shift in $Y_p$ is approximately $1.1\sigma $}{figure.14}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Comparison of CMB $TT$ (left) and $EE$ (right) power spectrum residuals for PTUM proxy-fluid dark radiation ($\sigma =0$, solid) and free-streaming dark radiation ($\sigma \neq  0$, dashed) at $\Delta N=0.10$. The free-streaming component produces a characteristic phase shift of the acoustic peaks, while the proxy-fluid shifts peak amplitudes without displacing their positions. The distinction is especially pronounced in $EE$ at $\ell >1000$. \emph  {(Author-reported figure; modified CLASS code not yet public.)}}}{41}{figure.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fluid_vs_fs}{{15}{41}{Comparison of CMB $TT$ (left) and $EE$ (right) power spectrum residuals for PTUM proxy-fluid dark radiation ($\sigma =0$, solid) and free-streaming dark radiation ($\sigma \neq 0$, dashed) at $\Delta N=0.10$. The free-streaming component produces a characteristic phase shift of the acoustic peaks, while the proxy-fluid shifts peak amplitudes without displacing their positions. The distinction is especially pronounced in $EE$ at $\ell >1000$. \emph {(Author-reported figure; modified CLASS code not yet public.)}}{figure.15}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Comparison of marginalised posterior distributions for $\Lambda $CDM (blue) and PTUM (red) from the full data combination. The standard parameters are consistent between the two models. PTUM shows a mild broadening in $\omega _{\rm  cdm}$ and $H_0$ due to the degeneracy with $\Delta N_{\rm  fluid}$, and a systematic upward shift of $H_0$ by $\sim 0.8\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm  {km\,s^{-1}\,Mpc^{-1}}$. \emph  {(Author-reported; chains not yet public.)}}}{42}{figure.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:lcdm_vs_ptum}{{16}{42}{Comparison of marginalised posterior distributions for $\Lambda $CDM (blue) and PTUM (red) from the full data combination. The standard parameters are consistent between the two models. PTUM shows a mild broadening in $\omega _{\rm cdm}$ and $H_0$ due to the degeneracy with $\Delta N_{\rm fluid}$, and a systematic upward shift of $H_0$ by $\sim 0.8\;\mathrm {km\,s^{-1}\,Mpc^{-1}}$. \emph {(Author-reported; chains not yet public.)}}{figure.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Linear perturbation stability diagnostics for all PTUM sectors. Top: squared sound speed $c_s^2$ as a function of scale factor, confirming $c_s^2>0$ (no gradient instability) throughout. Bottom: effective squared mass confirming the absence of tachyonic instabilities. All sectors are ghost-free by construction. \emph  {(Author-reported figure; modified CLASS code not yet public.)}}}{43}{figure.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:stability}{{17}{43}{Linear perturbation stability diagnostics for all PTUM sectors. Top: squared sound speed $c_s^2$ as a function of scale factor, confirming $c_s^2>0$ (no gradient instability) throughout. Bottom: effective squared mass confirming the absence of tachyonic instabilities. All sectors are ghost-free by construction. \emph {(Author-reported figure; modified CLASS code not yet public.)}}{figure.17}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces EFT validity diagnostics for PTUM. The inflationary Hubble scale $H_*$, potential energy $V_0^{1/4}$, slow-roll parameters $\epsilon $ and $|\eta |$, and coupling constants are shown as functions of $\lambda $. All quantities satisfy EFT consistency: $H_*\ll M_{\rm  Pl}$, $V_0<M_{\rm  Pl}^4$, $\epsilon ,|\eta |\ll 1$, and perturbative couplings. For $\lambda \lesssim 0.05$ the field excursion is super-Planckian, protectable by an approximate shift symmetry~\cite  {Freese1990,Silverstein2008}.}}{43}{figure.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:eft}{{18}{43}{EFT validity diagnostics for PTUM. The inflationary Hubble scale $H_*$, potential energy $V_0^{1/4}$, slow-roll parameters $\epsilon $ and $|\eta |$, and coupling constants are shown as functions of $\lambda $. All quantities satisfy EFT consistency: $H_*\ll M_{\rm Pl}$, $V_0<M_{\rm Pl}^4$, $\epsilon ,|\eta |\ll 1$, and perturbative couplings. For $\lambda \lesssim 0.05$ the field excursion is super-Planckian, protectable by an approximate shift symmetry~\cite {Freese1990,Silverstein2008}}{figure.18}{}}
